WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.473
Rivers of stone, deep peat, and drowned valleys.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.562
Stone runs are the Falklands' signature landform: streams and sheets of quartzite boulders,

00:00:10.562 --> 00:00:16.023
sometimes several kilometres long, filling valley floors with little soil between them.

00:00:16.023 --> 00:00:20.291
Charles Darwin described them in 1833 and puzzled over their origin.

00:00:21.391 --> 00:00:24.498
Most researchers see them as relics of a colder past.

00:00:24.498 --> 00:00:29.246
Frost shattered the quartzite, and the blocks crept downslope over frozen ground.

00:00:29.246 --> 00:00:32.470
Later, finer material was washed out from between them.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:37.570
Dating of exposed boulder surfaces suggests the largest runs built up over several cold

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.501
stages, perhaps over seven hundred thousand years.

00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:44.343
The islands were never covered by an ice sheet,

00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:47.726
though small glaciers carved hollows on the highest peaks.

00:00:47.726 --> 00:00:52.102
Peat, sometimes several metres deep, blankets much of the land: it is fuel,

00:00:52.102 --> 00:00:54.960
carbon store and an archive of past environments.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.702
And the coast is cut by long creeks and sounds,

00:00:57.702 --> 00:01:00.618
valleys drowned as the sea rose after the ice age.
